FreeBSD-SA-26:74.ktls                                       Security Advisory
                                                          The FreeBSD Project

Topic:          Kernel memory disclosure via KTLS control messages

Category:       core
Module:         ktls
Announced:      2026-11-17
Credits:        Olivier Cochard-Labbé
Affects:        All supported versions of FreeBSD.
Corrected:      2026-11-12 19:15:39 UTC (stable/15, 15.2-STABLE)
                2026-11-17 17:08:02 UTC (releng/15.1, 15.1-RELEASE-p7)
                2026-11-12 17:52:38 UTC (stable/14, 14.5-STABLE)
                2026-11-17 17:05:02 UTC (releng/14.5, 14.5-RELEASE-p4)
                2026-11-17 17:04:04 UTC (releng/14.4, 14.4-RELEASE-p13)
CVE Name:       CVE-2026-101313

For general information regarding FreeBSD Security Advisories,
including descriptions of the fields above, security branches, and the
following sections, please visit <URL:https://security.FreeBSD.org/>.

I.   Background

Kernel TLS (KTLS) moves Transport Layer Security (TLS) record processing
into the kernel, allowing applications to encrypt and decrypt socket data
without copying it to and from userspace and to serve TLS data with
sendfile(2).  Applications using KTLS on the transmit path may specify
the TLS record type for each outgoing record via a TLS_SET_RECORD_TYPE
control message passed to sendmsg(2).

II.  Problem Description

The code in sosend_generic() which parses TLS_SET_RECORD_TYPE control
messages failed to verify that the message was large enough to hold
its one-byte payload.  A control message containing only a cmsghdr
would pass the size check, causing the kernel to read one byte past
the end of the message and use it as the TLS record type.

III. Impact

A local user with a KTLS-enabled socket can send a crafted
TLS_SET_RECORD_TYPE control message to cause the kernel to read
one byte of uninitialized kernel memory and use it as the TLS
record type in an outgoing TLS record.  For TLS 1.2 connections,
the record type is visible in the plaintext record header,
constituting a kernel information disclosure.

